WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:06.842
[ Watch on KissFlix.co ]

2
00:07.112 --> 00:09.114
[theme music playing]

3
00:30.135 --> 00:34.223
YOU ARE MY DESTINY

4
00:38.227 --> 00:39.770
[intense music playing]

5
00:45.985 --> 00:46.986
Hello, everyone.

6
00:47.069 --> 00:48.946
I'm your host, Captain Cook,

7
00:49.029 --> 00:52.157
your K-food ambassador on The Table: K-Chef!

8
00:52.241 --> 00:54.827
-[laughs] -[cheering]

9
01:01.417 --> 01:03.627
[intense music continues playing]

10
01:09.842 --> 01:11.886
First, I'd like to thank all of our chefs

11
01:11.969 --> 01:15.472
for making the long trip to Haeon, thank you.

12
01:15.556 --> 01:19.727
As you all may know, Haeon is one of Korea's most pristine regions,

13
01:19.810 --> 01:23.439
known for clear skies, luxurious nature and beautiful scenery.

14
01:23.522 --> 01:25.357
Also known for its clean water.

15
01:25.441 --> 01:26.859
Since the Joseon era,

16
01:26.942 --> 01:29.820
traditional home-brewed liquors produced by its local families

17
01:29.904 --> 01:31.655
have earned nationwide acclaim.

18
01:31.739 --> 01:35.242
In particular, its pebble beach, which stretches for a whole kilometer,

19
01:35.326 --> 01:37.995
is covered in black pebbles rather than the typical white sand,

20
01:38.078 --> 01:42.708
creating a uniquely beautiful landscape that never fails to impress.

21
01:42.791 --> 01:46.921
Haeon also lies on the border of the Southern Gyeongsang and Jeolla Provinces,

22
01:47.004 --> 01:50.716
where the geographical, climatic and cultural characteristics

23
01:50.799 --> 01:52.426
of both regions come together.

24
01:52.509 --> 01:54.136
Thanks to those distinctive qualities,

25
01:54.219 --> 01:57.890
Haeon developed a rich cultural and artistic foundation.

26
01:57.973 --> 02:02.269
In fact, many traditional Korean artisans and artists were born and raised here.

27
02:02.353 --> 02:05.230
So today, we'll be holding the competition

28
02:05.314 --> 02:08.233
at a place with special cultural and historic significance.

29
02:09.777 --> 02:14.573
This outdoor stage has been set up at an elementary school with a long history.

30
02:14.657 --> 02:16.784
Yes, that's right…

31
02:18.994 --> 02:20.955
Haeon Elementary School!

32
02:21.038 --> 02:23.540
[audience cheering, applauding]

33
02:33.050 --> 02:34.969
A prestigious elementary school

34
02:35.052 --> 02:38.472
with a long history and many distinguished alumni.

35
02:38.555 --> 02:44.478
But as urbanization has drawn people away, Haeon's population has steadily declined.

36
02:44.561 --> 02:45.854
And sadly,

37
02:45.938 --> 02:49.149
Haeon Elementary School will close after this year.

38
02:50.109 --> 02:53.112
Elementary schools used to be called "national schools" in Korea,

39
02:53.946 --> 02:56.949
because virtually the entire population attended them.

40
02:57.032 --> 02:59.910
Some people may have not gone to middle school or high school,

41
02:59.994 --> 03:03.038
but almost everyone has attended elementary school.

42
03:03.122 --> 03:06.250
In that sense, don't you think elementary school is

43
03:06.333 --> 03:08.252
an inseparable part of our lives?

44
03:09.503 --> 03:11.839
Almost like it's destiny, wouldn't you say?

45
03:13.173 --> 03:15.884
YOU ARE MY DESTINY

46
03:20.431 --> 03:25.519
Yes, "You Are My Destiny" is the theme for this round.

47
03:25.602 --> 03:28.731
[sucks teeth] Perhaps some of you have already guessed

48
03:28.814 --> 03:31.233
what this round's challenge will be about.

49
03:32.609 --> 03:33.610
Hold on.

50
03:33.694 --> 03:37.364
Is it possible that someone has figured it out?

51
03:37.448 --> 03:40.034
[laughing]

52
03:40.117 --> 03:43.412
Oh, good. Fortunately, it seems no one has figured it out yet.

53
03:43.495 --> 03:45.831
-[audience laughing] -After all, if you did that too soon,

54
03:45.914 --> 03:49.334
our production crew, who racked their brains coming up with the theme,

55
03:49.418 --> 03:52.337
will be pretty crushed. [laughing]

56
03:52.963 --> 03:57.968
My dear cooks, without further ado, let's reveal the challenge.

57
03:58.052 --> 04:02.890
The challenge for the theme that's been on your minds all week long is…

58
04:02.973 --> 04:05.350
[dramatic music playing]

59
04:12.483 --> 04:13.859
…this!

60
04:13.942 --> 04:15.861
A MEAL THAT PAIRS PERFECTLY WITH TRADITIONAL KOREAN LIQUOR

61
04:29.541 --> 04:32.795
Jib by the audience, swing wide toward the right side.

62
04:32.878 --> 04:33.921
Pick up the pace.

63
04:51.271 --> 04:55.150
And for this challenge, we have a very special guest with us.

64
04:55.859 --> 04:57.736
She conceived this competition,

65
04:57.820 --> 05:00.739
and she's also an alumna of Haeon Elementary School.

66
05:01.657 --> 05:02.658
Everyone,

67
05:03.242 --> 05:05.160
let's give a warm welcome to

68
05:05.244 --> 05:07.579
Moon Hae-seon, Chairwoman of Pyeongyeon Group.

69
05:07.663 --> 05:09.706
Let's give her a round of applause!

70
05:09.790 --> 05:12.000
[scattered cheering]

71
05:19.341 --> 05:21.343
JANG OK-SEON OWNER-CHEF OF ONGOJISIM

72
05:21.426 --> 05:23.929
[laughs] A pairing with traditional liquor?

73
05:24.012 --> 05:25.639
I was thrilled to hear that.

74
05:26.390 --> 05:31.603
It finally feels like we have a challenge worthy of Korean cuisine, or K-food.

75
05:31.687 --> 05:32.688
DID YOU EXPECT THIS?

76
05:32.771 --> 05:34.439
Did I expect it?

77
05:34.523 --> 05:36.191
Honestly, no, not at all.

78
05:36.275 --> 05:37.943
It's a difficult theme, yes.

79
05:39.027 --> 05:42.197
But I think it is meaningful. Globalizing Korean cuisine.

80
05:42.281 --> 05:46.451
To take K-food global, we need to elevate the cuisine,

81
05:46.535 --> 05:48.704
and pairing it with alcohol is an essential part of that.

82
05:48.787 --> 05:52.624
Of course, K-food is already drawing attention around the world,

83
05:53.167 --> 05:56.378
but for a country's cuisine to truly step up to the global stage,

84
05:56.461 --> 05:59.214
it must reach a higher level of sophistication.

85
05:59.298 --> 06:03.886
Pairing it with alcohol is one of the key steps toward that goal.

86
06:03.969 --> 06:07.014
Alcohol is about more than just partying and getting drunk.

87
06:07.097 --> 06:08.599
CHA WOO-JIN EXECUTIVE CHEF OF EXCEPTION

88
06:08.682 --> 06:10.642
Uh… when paired with food,

89
06:10.726 --> 06:14.605
it can bring out a dish's strengths and compensate for its weaknesses.

90
06:14.688 --> 06:15.689
[inhales sharply]

91
06:15.772 --> 06:17.900
I believe French wine and Japanese sake

92
06:17.983 --> 06:21.069
have developed alongside their respective cuisines,

93
06:21.153 --> 06:23.488
creating synergy between food and drink.

94
06:23.572 --> 06:24.990
I think it's fair to say…

95
06:25.073 --> 06:26.283
JOSHUA PAYTON OWNER-CHEF OF HUSH

96
06:26.366 --> 06:29.119
…that pairing food with alcohol has helped advance

97
06:29.203 --> 06:32.206
not only cuisine and alcoholic beverages, but culture itself.

98
06:32.289 --> 06:34.041
With that in mind, for this round,

99
06:34.124 --> 06:39.171
we present a challenge unlike anything that's ever been attempted before.

100
06:41.632 --> 06:43.634
You'll prepare a meal

101
06:43.717 --> 06:47.721
that pairs so perfectly with traditional Korean liquor that it feels like destiny.

102
06:47.804 --> 06:49.806
PARK JE-HYEON OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

103
06:49.890 --> 06:51.767
[exhales deeply] Traditional liquor…

104
06:51.850 --> 06:53.602
[sucks teeth]

105
06:53.685 --> 06:55.103
Traditional Korean liquor…

106
06:55.187 --> 06:56.438
CHRIS K. HEAD CHEF OF FAMIGLIA

107
06:56.521 --> 06:58.315
I don't know much about our liquors.

108
06:59.274 --> 07:02.903
I think I've used things like Korean black raspberry wine a few times.

109
07:02.986 --> 07:05.572
Transform the street food of your school days

110
07:05.656 --> 07:08.659
into sophisticated dishes that pair well with liquor.

111
07:08.742 --> 07:10.244
YANG JA-RYONG HEAD CHEF OF GOLDEN DRAGON

112
07:10.327 --> 07:13.163
[sighs] Come on, is it really street food?

113
07:13.247 --> 07:15.832
That's things like sundaes and tteokbokki.

114
07:15.916 --> 07:18.543
Man, if I'd known this was coming,

115
07:18.627 --> 07:20.671
I would've gone to Korean snack bars more often.

116
07:20.754 --> 07:22.172
ISABEL DOMINGUEZ HEAD CHEF OF INTEGRAL

117
07:22.256 --> 07:23.548
I'm just grateful.

118
07:24.675 --> 07:26.426
Honestly, I was really bummed out about it.

119
07:26.510 --> 07:30.264
I'd like to thank the production crew and the other chefs

120
07:30.347 --> 07:34.184
for… for allowing me to return to the competition.

121
07:34.268 --> 07:37.104
And, of course, I'm most grateful to Chef Joshua

122
07:37.938 --> 07:39.523
for inviting me to join his team.

123
07:39.606 --> 07:41.483
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026 ROUND 3: YOU ARE MY DESTINY

124
07:42.067 --> 07:44.361
[Captain Cook] This round has two challenges.

125
07:44.444 --> 07:46.280
Create two dishes that pair well

126
07:46.363 --> 07:48.824
with traditional liquor produced in this region.

127
07:48.907 --> 07:51.159
Prepare a meal that makes the perfect pairing.

128
07:51.243 --> 07:54.579
Base the dishes on street food from your memories.

129
07:54.663 --> 07:56.498
Elevate Korean snack food.

130
08:07.801 --> 08:08.802
[grunts]

131
08:14.474 --> 08:17.936
Wow, is this the Pyeongyeon Training Center?

132
08:18.020 --> 08:19.813
I mean, it's in the middle of nowhere.

133
08:20.897 --> 08:22.607
They really went all out, huh?

134
08:22.691 --> 08:24.693
[Je-hyeon] It's like a luxury hotel.

135
08:24.776 --> 08:26.862
They must take good care of this place.

136
08:26.945 --> 08:28.071
Looks like it.

137
08:35.370 --> 08:37.914
PYEONGYEON GROUP

138
08:38.707 --> 08:40.083
Wow.

139
08:40.167 --> 08:43.086
They said we'd be staying in a suite. Not bad at all.

140
08:43.920 --> 08:45.797
There's two living rooms in here.

141
08:45.881 --> 08:47.883
And we have our own bathrooms.

142
08:47.966 --> 08:50.302
A whole kitchen too. This is nice.

143
08:50.385 --> 08:53.263
The rooms all seem to be the same size and layout.

144
08:53.347 --> 08:55.974
We'll take the two rooms by the living room over there.

145
08:58.643 --> 08:59.728
Let's check it out.

146
08:59.811 --> 09:00.812
Yes, Chef.

147
09:12.115 --> 09:13.116
[sniffles]

148
09:32.344 --> 09:33.929
-Hey. -Hey.

149
09:35.639 --> 09:38.433
[chuckles] Quite old-fashioned, yeah?

150
09:38.517 --> 09:40.477
[chuckles] What is it?

151
09:41.228 --> 09:43.647
Hmm. [gasps] A lucky charm?

152
09:44.689 --> 09:45.690
Oh…

153
09:46.400 --> 09:47.651
No, it's not that.

154
09:49.611 --> 09:53.156
My birth mother wrapped this scarf around my neck the last time we saw each other.

155
09:54.699 --> 09:57.369
Turns out my American adoptive parents

156
09:58.370 --> 10:00.122
kept it safe all this time.

157
10:01.415 --> 10:02.457
Oh. [sighs]

158
10:02.541 --> 10:04.751
Oh, there's nothing special to it.

159
10:04.835 --> 10:08.255
It's just that when I have it with me…

160
10:09.840 --> 10:11.508
I guess I feel a little more at ease.

161
10:13.093 --> 10:14.928
[chuckles]

162
10:17.973 --> 10:19.474
[Song-yi] Tell me.

163
10:19.558 --> 10:21.101
What on earth are you thinking?

164
10:22.644 --> 10:24.062
Don't pretend you don't know.

165
10:24.146 --> 10:25.439
Why did you choose me?

166
10:25.522 --> 10:28.608
You should've picked Chef Geon-woo, who's way more experienced.

167
10:29.359 --> 10:31.736
Are you even trying to win this competition?

168
10:34.156 --> 10:36.783
I'd also like to hear that answer.

169
10:38.243 --> 10:41.079
What do you want with a novice who doesn't even know…

170
10:42.664 --> 10:43.957
the basics of cooking?

171
10:50.714 --> 10:53.133
[Song-yi] Honestly, I have no idea what I'm supposed to do.

172
10:53.216 --> 10:54.801
CHOI SONG-YI MANAGER OF FAMIGLIA/JUNIOR CHEF

173
10:54.885 --> 10:56.636
As you already know, I'm not actually a professional chef.

174
10:56.720 --> 10:58.597
I don't have the skills, and I don't know much.

175
10:58.680 --> 11:01.349
I'm basically dead weight. A total burden.

176
11:02.809 --> 11:06.897
[sighs] So why on earth did Chris choose me as a cook on this round?

177
11:07.772 --> 11:11.943
They said they'd reveal which Korean liquor we'll use on the day of the shoot.

178
11:12.027 --> 11:16.072
[inhales sharply] That means that the selection process is part of the task.

179
11:17.866 --> 11:18.867
I agree.

180
11:20.160 --> 11:22.120
There's a good chance the clue in the envelope

181
11:22.787 --> 11:24.789
is also related to Korean liquor.

182
11:27.167 --> 11:29.169
[tense music playing]

183
11:44.434 --> 11:48.063
I even wondered for a minute if he was trying to mess with me.

184
11:48.146 --> 11:50.315
But why would he want to mess with me?

185
11:50.398 --> 11:53.944
Or maybe he doesn't want to compete anymore.

186
11:54.027 --> 11:56.196
Maybe he wants to go back to the States.

187
11:57.489 --> 11:59.032
If that's not it either…

188
12:02.327 --> 12:03.370
He's lost his mind?

189
12:05.455 --> 12:08.416
CLUE: CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

190
12:08.500 --> 12:10.377
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

191
12:15.757 --> 12:18.343
Does anything come to mind? Let's hear.

192
12:18.426 --> 12:19.427
None at all.

193
12:22.347 --> 12:23.515
That phrase.

194
12:23.598 --> 12:25.850
Isn't it from The Tale of Hong Gil-dong?

195
12:26.977 --> 12:27.978
Yeah, that's right.

196
12:28.061 --> 12:30.438
Hong Gil-dong was a bastard son,

197
12:30.522 --> 12:34.484
so there's this bit about him not being able to address his father as "Father."

198
12:34.568 --> 12:38.113
So, does the liquor have to do with The Tale of Hong Gil-dong?

199
12:39.155 --> 12:40.156
You think so?

200
12:40.240 --> 12:43.618
[inhales deeply] Should we look up what kind of liquor appears in the tale?

201
12:43.702 --> 12:44.911
[Je-hyeon] No.

202
12:44.995 --> 12:47.372
They wouldn't have given us such a simple clue.

203
12:47.455 --> 12:48.498
This production team

204
12:50.125 --> 12:51.626
doesn't make things that easy for us.

205
12:51.710 --> 12:54.879
It's really hard to pair anything with traditional Korean liquor.

206
12:56.089 --> 12:59.759
Makgeolli is easy to drink, but it's-it's fizzy and light-bodied,

207
12:59.843 --> 13:03.263
so I find… I find it pairs well with casual dining.

208
13:03.346 --> 13:06.766
[inhales sharply] Liquors made with medicinal herbs have strong aroma.

209
13:06.850 --> 13:08.852
I feel like they overpower the food.

210
13:09.436 --> 13:12.230
Most of our traditional liquors are like that…

211
13:13.356 --> 13:15.609
Should we look into distilled liquor instead?

212
13:24.784 --> 13:27.662
-What do you think it means? -[scoffs]

213
13:27.746 --> 13:30.540
I'm very sorry. I have no idea, Chef.

214
13:33.168 --> 13:35.086
I… [sighs]

215
13:35.170 --> 13:39.174
…only know Chinese liquor, so I'm no help at all.

216
13:41.426 --> 13:43.762
It's frustrating.

217
13:45.055 --> 13:46.723
Still,

218
13:46.806 --> 13:50.226
aren't Japanese liquors more similar to ours anyway?

219
13:50.310 --> 13:52.979
-They have their own soju too. -Suppose so.

220
13:53.938 --> 13:55.857
Mr. Park. You're Japanese.

221
13:55.940 --> 13:59.194
You must know Japanese liquor like the back of your hand, isn't that right?

222
13:59.944 --> 14:01.529
[laughing]

223
14:02.280 --> 14:05.909
Well, Mr. Park, you have a much bigger advantage

224
14:05.992 --> 14:08.662
than all of us do in this challenge, so you should--

225
14:08.745 --> 14:10.163
Stop it, I'm not Japanese.

226
14:14.584 --> 14:17.754
My great-grandfather was forcibly taken to Japan during the Japanese colonial period.

227
14:17.837 --> 14:19.297
PARK JE-HYEON OWNER-CHEF OF BINGSAK

228
14:19.381 --> 14:22.759
My grandfather was born there, and my family has been there ever since.

229
14:22.842 --> 14:25.595
I'm a fourth-generation Korean-Japanese.

230
14:27.972 --> 14:30.016
My grandmother is Japanese.

231
14:31.601 --> 14:35.772
So I'm of Korean and Japanese descent actually.

232
14:36.481 --> 14:39.734
So in Japan, I was always teased for being half-and-half.

233
14:40.944 --> 14:43.029
Even now that I've become a Korean citizen,

234
14:44.614 --> 14:46.658
people still see me as half-and-half.

235
14:46.741 --> 14:48.493
But… [inhales deeply]

236
14:49.911 --> 14:51.663
…my roots are Korean.

237
14:51.746 --> 14:54.416
My father always used to tell me,

238
14:55.375 --> 14:56.376
"Never

239
14:57.419 --> 14:59.546
forget that you are Korean."

240
14:59.629 --> 15:01.548
Then how about jeongjong?

241
15:03.800 --> 15:06.052
I remember seeing my mother put out jeongjong

242
15:06.136 --> 15:08.138
for ancestral rites when I was young.

243
15:08.221 --> 15:10.140
Jeongjong has a mild and clean taste,

244
15:10.223 --> 15:13.476
so I feel like it pairs well with almost any kind of street food.

245
15:14.144 --> 15:16.855
Wouldn't it be great for pairing with other foods?

246
15:17.731 --> 15:19.023
You want to use Masamune sake?

247
15:19.941 --> 15:21.735
-Sorry? -[scoffs]

248
15:23.069 --> 15:24.070
Both of you.

249
15:26.489 --> 15:27.866
Do you know what K-food is?

250
15:29.784 --> 15:34.831
I didn't know jeongjong was actually Japanese sake, introduced into Korea.

251
15:35.707 --> 15:38.042
Um, it was used for ancestral rites…

252
15:39.377 --> 15:41.421
So I just assumed it was a Korean drink.

253
15:42.046 --> 15:45.884
What's important is getting information about this region.

254
15:46.801 --> 15:49.971
What the local specialties are, which ingredients are well-known,

255
15:50.054 --> 15:51.639
and what the locals like to eat.

256
15:51.723 --> 15:53.933
We could start by looking around the market.

257
15:54.017 --> 15:56.644
Since we're by the sea, we should check out the fish market too.

258
15:56.728 --> 15:59.731
We should also visit snack bars near the schools.

259
15:59.814 --> 16:00.857
Anyway, remember this.

260
16:01.399 --> 16:05.361
If any of us makes even one small mistake, both teams get eliminated.

261
16:10.617 --> 16:11.701
Don't you worry.

262
16:13.036 --> 16:14.913
We won't lose this.

263
16:17.081 --> 16:18.458
[Jeong-won] A live broadcast?

264
16:18.541 --> 16:20.710
[Hyeon-ju] Yes. A live broadcast.

265
16:21.711 --> 16:26.549
You're saying you want this Sunday's shoot to be broadcast live?

266
16:27.717 --> 16:29.928
It's not that complicated.

267
16:30.011 --> 16:33.515
We're filming it anyway, so let's just make it more dramatic by going live.

268
16:33.598 --> 16:38.311
Ma'am, the thing is we have less than four days to prepare.

269
16:38.394 --> 16:40.647
Isn't that cutting it a little close, Ms. Baek?

270
16:41.856 --> 16:44.567
I'll send over some backup and a broadcast crew.

271
16:45.276 --> 16:46.694
[scoffs]

272
16:48.404 --> 16:51.574
[shouts] What's with everyone? Isn't this a killer idea?

273
16:51.658 --> 16:55.954
With ratings and buzz both climbing, now's the time to be bold and go live

274
16:56.037 --> 16:58.540
and really crank up that reality factor!

275
16:58.623 --> 17:01.376
We'll air the scheduled episode at its usual time,

276
17:01.459 --> 17:03.169
then go straight into a live broadcast.

277
17:03.253 --> 17:05.129
"You Are My Destiny," part two!

278
17:05.213 --> 17:07.799
I guarantee it. The ratings for this?

279
17:08.466 --> 17:09.592
It will double!

280
17:10.218 --> 17:11.803
Double! Double!

281
17:11.886 --> 17:13.930
Double! [trilling, cackles]

282
17:14.013 --> 17:17.100
[scoffs] How do we change the schedule with such short notice?

283
17:17.183 --> 17:19.060
We'd have to pull all the shows from those time slots.

284
17:19.143 --> 17:22.105
-And the advertisers for those shows-- -Listen, Ms. Seon.

285
17:23.606 --> 17:25.275
I'm the head of this network.

286
17:25.358 --> 17:28.361
You think anyone would object when I'm the one making the decision?

287
17:31.030 --> 17:32.031
Huh?

288
17:32.824 --> 17:34.742
[Geon-woo] Apparently, that's when it began.

289
17:34.826 --> 17:37.829
Distilled liquor was produced during the Goryeo era.

290
17:37.912 --> 17:40.874
Then many years go by, and we come to the Joseon era…

291
17:40.957 --> 17:42.458
You could say the golden age of liquor

292
17:42.542 --> 17:44.043
-began in the Joseon era… -Chef Geon-woo really made

293
17:44.127 --> 17:45.503
all kinds of stuff.

294
17:45.587 --> 17:47.380
-The liquors produced back then… -I know.

295
17:47.463 --> 17:50.508
I knew he had a traditional Korean liquor sommelier certification,

296
17:50.592 --> 17:52.343
but to have a whole channel about it…

297
17:54.262 --> 17:58.224
[sighs] I knew that you should've picked him instead.

298
18:00.059 --> 18:03.313
[Geon-woo] So individual households began brewing their own liquor…

299
18:04.105 --> 18:07.483
And they called it… That's right. Home-brewed liquor.

300
18:07.567 --> 18:11.029
They were made using different techniques, and they all tasted different too.

301
18:11.112 --> 18:13.406
In other words, liquor wasn't just liquor.

302
18:13.489 --> 18:16.159
It was part of the family's culture and craftsmanship.

303
18:16.659 --> 18:20.079
There were all kinds of varieties too. For example, soju--

304
18:24.709 --> 18:26.502
[chuckling] To be honest,

305
18:26.586 --> 18:29.547
apart from soju and makgeolli,

306
18:29.631 --> 18:33.676
I don't think I've ever really tried other Korean liquor in my life before.

307
18:34.177 --> 18:37.180
But you know, watching those videos has piqued my interest.

308
18:37.263 --> 18:40.016
Now I'm really curious about what those liquors taste like.

309
18:40.099 --> 18:41.100
Exactly.

310
18:41.184 --> 18:44.520
Korean liquor could pair surprisingly well with all kinds of food.

311
18:44.604 --> 18:47.190
Like appetizers, mains, desserts… everything.

312
18:47.273 --> 18:48.858
-Right. -[Song-yi gasps]

313
18:48.942 --> 18:50.234
It's really nice out.

314
18:50.318 --> 18:53.029
Why don't we pick up some traditional liquors and snacks at lunchtime

315
18:53.112 --> 18:55.406
and have a tasting together on the beach? What do you think?

316
18:55.490 --> 18:57.742
-I'd love that. -[Woo-jin] Hey.

317
18:58.910 --> 19:00.286
Excuse me.

318
19:00.370 --> 19:01.996
Are you here to have fun?

319
19:02.830 --> 19:04.457
Are you on vacation?

320
19:05.959 --> 19:06.960
No, I just--

321
19:07.043 --> 19:08.127
If you have time to spare,

322
19:08.211 --> 19:10.254
go practice your knife skills in the kitchen.

323
19:11.547 --> 19:15.385
And don't drag the other competitors down. Do you even think at all? Seriously.

324
19:15.468 --> 19:17.512
[Han] Aren't you being a bit too harsh?

325
19:20.014 --> 19:22.350
You know she's our teammate for this round.

326
19:30.650 --> 19:32.443
What do you know about cooking?

327
19:33.945 --> 19:34.946
What?

328
19:35.029 --> 19:36.739
Have you poached anything?

329
19:36.823 --> 19:38.866
Confit? You don't know what sous vide is.

330
19:38.950 --> 19:42.996
Have you ever made fresh pasta dough? How many kinds of pasta can you name?

331
19:43.871 --> 19:47.709
Agar, xanthan gum and maltodextrin. Do you know what any of those are?

332
19:54.173 --> 19:57.051
Do you know what I really hate?

333
20:01.264 --> 20:03.182
Cleaning up after amateurs.

334
20:04.600 --> 20:06.310
Amateurs who don't

335
20:07.311 --> 20:08.396
have what it takes.

336
20:11.566 --> 20:13.317
[sighs]

337
20:13.401 --> 20:15.028
How about you clean up…

338
20:16.571 --> 20:17.655
your own mess,

339
20:18.823 --> 20:19.824
Chris K.?

340
20:19.907 --> 20:22.910
If you make even the slightest mistake this round,

341
20:22.994 --> 20:25.246
or if we get eliminated because of you two…

342
20:27.915 --> 20:30.043
I won't let you off lightly.

343
20:35.715 --> 20:36.966
[chuckling]

344
20:42.722 --> 20:44.057
You're laughing?

345
20:47.727 --> 20:48.728
[sighs]

346
20:51.647 --> 20:53.232
"Mind your

347
20:53.816 --> 20:57.070
own business, man."

348
20:58.780 --> 21:00.281
[scoffs]

349
21:01.115 --> 21:03.868
That was a line from a movie I saw a few days ago.

350
21:04.619 --> 21:05.912
I thought it was funny.

351
21:07.288 --> 21:10.083
-[laughing] -What? What did you say?

352
21:10.875 --> 21:12.251
I'll leave you to it.

353
21:19.717 --> 21:22.303
Damn it. That bastard keeps talking down to me.

354
21:24.555 --> 21:26.307
And he's a year younger. [scoffs]

355
21:27.725 --> 21:29.435
[museum staff] Nuruk is the main ingredient.

356
21:29.519 --> 21:30.895
HAEON TRADITIONAL LIQUOR MUSEUM

357
21:30.978 --> 21:32.146
And what makes this liquor special is

358
21:32.230 --> 21:35.316
that it's distilled once more after fermentation.

359
21:36.234 --> 21:37.318
So it's very clear,

360
21:37.401 --> 21:39.654
but the alcohol content is relatively high…

361
21:46.202 --> 21:47.620
What do you think?

362
21:49.831 --> 21:52.208
It has a really subtle, distinctive aroma.

363
21:52.291 --> 21:53.709
-I like it. Wow. -Right?

364
21:58.673 --> 22:01.592
"Ryu. Smoky."

365
22:03.261 --> 22:04.262
Have a taste.

366
22:43.301 --> 22:48.764
Mmm. Okay. The tteokbokki sauce definitely gets rid of the gamey smell.

367
22:48.848 --> 22:52.518
They say the students like eating it this way. That's probably why.

368
22:57.690 --> 22:59.025
I looked it up online.

369
22:59.108 --> 23:02.153
In Gwangju, they dip their sundaes in chojang.

370
23:02.236 --> 23:04.614
In Busan, they dip it in makjang. And in Jeju, soy sauce.

371
23:05.198 --> 23:08.034
-That's interesting, don't you think? -[chuckles]

372
23:09.202 --> 23:10.369
Oh.

373
23:10.453 --> 23:12.205
[plastic rustling]

374
23:28.930 --> 23:29.931
Mmm.

375
23:30.973 --> 23:32.600
-How is it? -Hmm?

376
23:38.272 --> 23:39.857
Mmm.

377
23:39.941 --> 23:41.067
Just as I thought.

378
23:42.109 --> 23:44.320
The spiciness of gochujang and the sweetness of makgeolli

379
23:44.403 --> 23:45.613
don't clash much.

380
23:45.696 --> 23:49.242
Hmm. You're right. They actually complement each other.

381
23:49.325 --> 23:50.785
Oh, you think so?

382
23:57.959 --> 24:00.086
Mmm. Oh, you're right.

383
24:00.169 --> 24:03.547
[sucks teeth] So I don't think it would pair well

384
24:03.631 --> 24:06.133
with a dry, premium liquor other than makgeolli.

385
24:07.885 --> 24:10.388
What if we find a way to mellow out the gochujang?

386
24:10.972 --> 24:12.556
The theme is nostalgic street food,

387
24:12.640 --> 24:15.142
so leaving out gochujang tteokbokki feels a little…

388
24:15.226 --> 24:18.729
Uh, if the liquor we're given for the challenge is makgeolli, that's fine.

389
24:21.065 --> 24:22.400
But if it isn't…

390
24:27.863 --> 24:28.906
[Isabel] Let's go outside.

391
24:28.990 --> 24:32.159
How about we get some fresh air? We need some air.

392
24:32.243 --> 24:33.995
[chuckles] Okay.

393
24:38.749 --> 24:39.750
Huh?

394
24:40.835 --> 24:42.336
Chris?

395
24:43.129 --> 24:44.130
Hmm?

396
24:44.213 --> 24:45.923
[chuckles]

397
24:46.507 --> 24:48.509
Hmm. Hmm?

398
24:51.637 --> 24:52.638
It's beautiful.

399
24:53.723 --> 24:54.724
It is.

400
24:56.225 --> 24:58.561
There are plenty of beautiful beaches in South Carolina,

401
24:58.644 --> 25:01.689
but I've… I've never seen a pebble beach like this.

402
25:02.982 --> 25:04.150
It's quite small,

403
25:04.859 --> 25:06.569
but really unique.

404
25:06.652 --> 25:09.780
I've never been anywhere like this either. It's so nice.

405
25:16.912 --> 25:18.664
The pebbles here are so pretty.

406
25:18.748 --> 25:21.709
They're all smooth and round with no sharp edges.

407
25:21.792 --> 25:24.712
[gasps] I think we could use them for plating, don't you think?

408
25:28.341 --> 25:30.426
-[gentle music playing] -[breathes deeply]

409
25:36.474 --> 25:37.725
[sighs]

410
25:55.409 --> 25:56.410
Chris.

411
25:57.620 --> 25:58.829
Hmm?

412
26:00.331 --> 26:03.000
I heard you also left Korea as a child, like me.

413
26:03.876 --> 26:06.629
When you think about Korea, what comes to mind?

414
26:20.935 --> 26:23.854
I'm really sorry. That's too vague a question, right?

415
26:25.981 --> 26:30.361
I was really blown away when I tasted the food you made in rounds one and two.

416
26:31.195 --> 26:32.947
Not only were the concepts creative,

417
26:34.782 --> 26:37.284
-but they tasted amazing too. -[chuckles]

418
26:37.368 --> 26:40.287
I wondered what kind of experience and training it would take

419
26:40.371 --> 26:41.914
for someone to create food like that.

420
26:44.375 --> 26:45.376
[chuckles]

421
26:51.465 --> 26:52.716
What about you, Joshua?

422
26:55.261 --> 26:56.679
What comes to mind

423
26:58.013 --> 26:59.306
when you think of Korea?

424
27:06.480 --> 27:07.481
The color pink.

425
27:08.607 --> 27:09.650
The color pink?

426
27:19.994 --> 27:21.996
Honey, Mom will be right back.

427
27:23.330 --> 27:25.124
So stay put and don't move.

428
27:25.207 --> 27:26.709
Okay, Yeong-ho?

429
27:28.836 --> 27:30.129
[chuckles]

430
27:46.562 --> 27:47.563
Mom.

431
27:48.397 --> 27:49.398
Hmm?

432
27:49.982 --> 27:52.776
Will we go have jjajangmyeon when you get back?

433
27:54.403 --> 27:55.404
Jjajangmyeon?

434
27:55.488 --> 27:56.489
Yeah.

435
27:56.572 --> 27:58.616
I really want to have jjajangmyeon.

436
28:00.159 --> 28:02.620
And tangsuyuk too, Mom.

437
28:04.497 --> 28:06.290
Mmm. Okay.

438
28:06.373 --> 28:09.251
-Yay! I'm so excited. -[sobs]

439
28:10.377 --> 28:12.463
When I get back… [sniffles]

440
28:13.464 --> 28:14.507
…we'll have

441
28:15.341 --> 28:17.551
jjajangmyeon together, Yeong-ho.

442
28:18.344 --> 28:19.720
And tangsuyuk too.

443
28:22.264 --> 28:23.265
[cries]

444
28:27.228 --> 28:28.229
[sniffles]

445
28:31.607 --> 28:34.068
[melancholy music playing]

446
28:34.151 --> 28:37.613
[Joshua] That day, my mother didn't come back for me.

447
28:38.113 --> 28:40.032
When the park was closing,

448
28:40.115 --> 28:42.701
the park attendant took me to the police station.

449
28:45.371 --> 28:48.415
There was a note in my pocket that simply read,

450
28:48.499 --> 28:50.251
"Please take care of this kid."

451
28:52.628 --> 28:53.796
They never found her.

452
28:54.421 --> 28:58.759
Back in that time, these things used to happen a lot.

453
29:02.846 --> 29:04.431
Growing up, I used to wonder…

454
29:06.267 --> 29:10.312
maybe she didn't come back because I told her I wanted to eat something special.

455
29:11.438 --> 29:13.857
Maybe it was because I'd asked her for something expensive.

456
29:15.192 --> 29:16.860
I should've just kept quiet.

457
29:18.320 --> 29:20.155
I shouldn't have asked her for anything.

458
29:24.201 --> 29:25.661
Maybe then,

459
29:26.829 --> 29:28.372
she would've come back.

460
29:54.356 --> 29:56.108
[sighs]

461
29:56.191 --> 29:59.862
I mean, there's so much variety in the aromas and flavors.

462
29:59.945 --> 30:01.280
Why on earth

463
30:01.363 --> 30:05.159
don't supermarkets or convenience stores sell these delicious traditional liquors?

464
30:06.118 --> 30:08.537
They sell all kinds of soju and beer.

465
30:08.621 --> 30:10.456
[museum staff chuckles] Tell me about it.

466
30:10.539 --> 30:14.793
They'd be much easier to spot if they were sold through more outlets.

467
30:15.586 --> 30:18.172
And as you can see, they're not that expensive either.

468
30:18.255 --> 30:19.923
Here. Have a look at this one.

469
30:20.007 --> 30:23.969
Of course, there are some premium limited-edition bottles as well.

470
30:26.639 --> 30:27.640
Ah.

471
30:32.853 --> 30:35.189
[gentle music playing]

472
30:52.581 --> 30:53.874
"Jehyangju."

473
30:53.957 --> 30:55.959
[museum staff] That bottle is empty.

474
30:58.003 --> 31:01.840
We finished it and thought we'd keep the bottle as a souvenir.

475
31:01.924 --> 31:02.925
[chuckles]

476
31:06.470 --> 31:09.139
Have they… Have they stopped producing this one?

477
31:09.223 --> 31:10.599
Yeah.

478
31:10.683 --> 31:13.852
They shut down the brewery. Said it was too much work.

479
31:13.936 --> 31:16.647
We barely managed to get our hands on that bottle.

480
31:16.730 --> 31:18.440
We finally finished it two days ago,

481
31:18.524 --> 31:22.444
so I'm afraid that empty bottle is all that's left.

482
31:22.528 --> 31:23.570
Oh.

483
31:23.654 --> 31:25.698
It was an excellent home-brewed liquor

484
31:25.781 --> 31:28.033
that had been passed down through the Hamyang Park clan

485
31:28.117 --> 31:29.868
since the Unified Silla period.

486
31:31.036 --> 31:33.080
[sighs] It's a shame, really.

487
31:33.163 --> 31:35.874
But these things happen fairly often in the industry.

488
31:35.958 --> 31:37.167
I see.

489
31:43.424 --> 31:44.466
JEHYANGJU

490
32:03.026 --> 32:04.653
[magical music playing]

491
32:21.170 --> 32:22.880
[emotional music playing]

492
32:34.641 --> 32:37.853
[Song-yi] What is this… fresh, green scent?

493
32:49.782 --> 32:51.074
Lotus flowers.

494
32:52.284 --> 32:54.661
[gentle music playing]

495
33:17.559 --> 33:18.560
[inaudible]

496
33:35.244 --> 33:36.870
The scent of lotus flowers.

497
33:45.003 --> 33:47.798
[executive] The distilled liquor on the left launches next month.

498
33:47.881 --> 33:51.510
And the rice wine on the right launches a week later according to our plans.

499
33:51.593 --> 33:53.470
Uh, we developed this distilled liquor

500
33:53.554 --> 33:55.514
to give traditional liquor a more modern image

501
33:55.597 --> 33:59.226
with a fresh, clean taste that really appeals to younger consumers.

502
33:59.309 --> 34:00.936
It's 28% alcohol.

503
34:01.019 --> 34:02.271
The liquor on the right is yakju

504
34:02.354 --> 34:05.190
with a ginger and balloon flower root added during fermentation.

505
34:05.274 --> 34:10.362
Actually, we at Pyeongyeon F&B have particularly high hopes for this yakju.

506
34:10.445 --> 34:12.823
According to our internal data, over the past year,

507
34:12.906 --> 34:14.700
wine and beer consumption among people

508
34:14.783 --> 34:17.661
between the ages of 20 and 49 has dropped sharply.

509
34:17.744 --> 34:21.832
But consumption of soju and whiskey has remained unchanged.

510
34:21.915 --> 34:23.709
That suggests there was demand

511
34:23.792 --> 34:27.170
for a different low-alcohol option besides wine and beer.

512
34:27.254 --> 34:28.297
That's where we come in.

513
34:28.380 --> 34:29.381
[sighs]

514
34:29.464 --> 34:31.925
That's what led us to develop a traditional liquor

515
34:32.009 --> 34:34.011
with 15% alcohol for this launch.

516
34:34.094 --> 34:38.682
I understand the challenge for this round of The Table: K-Chef 2026 involves

517
34:38.765 --> 34:40.601
an assortment of Korean traditional liquor.

518
34:42.519 --> 34:44.146
If the challenge featured one of our releases,

519
34:44.229 --> 34:46.398
wouldn't that be great publicity for the company?

520
34:46.481 --> 34:47.733
It would be perfect for us.

521
34:47.816 --> 34:48.984
[sighs]

522
34:49.067 --> 34:52.696
So, if Ms. Seon could possibly coordinate with us in advance…

523
34:52.779 --> 34:54.489
[scoffs]

524
34:54.573 --> 34:55.949
If she does,

525
34:56.700 --> 34:59.786
people will say we used the show to promote our products.

526
35:00.370 --> 35:02.247
Do you think it would do our image any good?

527
35:02.831 --> 35:04.541
What day and age do you think it is?

528
35:04.625 --> 35:05.918
Consumers are way ahead of us!

529
35:06.501 --> 35:07.544
I'm sorry, Chairwoman.

530
35:07.628 --> 35:09.671
I agree with you wholeheartedly, Chairwoman.

531
35:10.881 --> 35:14.927
Ah. Unfortunately, there's no time to coordinate with F&B.

532
35:15.886 --> 35:18.388
And for the third round, a surprise.

533
35:19.097 --> 35:21.224
It will be broadcast live this Sunday.

534
35:21.308 --> 35:22.935
[executive 2] What?

535
35:24.102 --> 35:25.520
Live?

536
35:26.730 --> 35:29.691
What is this? What is this tacky reaction?

537
35:29.775 --> 35:31.234
Are you all that surprised?

538
35:31.318 --> 35:32.778
No need to worry.

539
35:32.861 --> 35:35.822
We've sorted out the advertising and programming arrangements internally.

540
35:35.906 --> 35:37.407
[Hae-seon] What do you mean?

541
35:37.491 --> 35:39.368
Everyone, use your heads for a second.

542
35:39.451 --> 35:42.287
Everyone is talking about The Table right now.

543
35:42.371 --> 35:46.291
Ratings climbing every episode, buzz index hitting new highs.

544
35:46.375 --> 35:49.169
And we've hit the highest ratings in our network's history. So, what do we do?

545
35:49.252 --> 35:51.421
We give our viewers the best possible show.

546
35:51.505 --> 35:53.632
No scripts, just raw and unedited.

547
35:53.715 --> 35:57.803
We show the chefs in action, right now in real time. Wow!

548
35:57.886 --> 36:00.555
Amazing! So fantastic!

549
36:00.639 --> 36:01.765
Look.

550
36:03.433 --> 36:07.104
This is exactly what viewers want from television these days.

551
36:07.187 --> 36:09.898
A reality show that feels more real than reality.

552
36:10.732 --> 36:13.527
I mean, people try things like this all the time on social media,

553
36:13.610 --> 36:17.823
so why, why can't Pyeongyeon Group even give it a real try?

554
36:20.325 --> 36:21.576
Don't you agree, Chairwoman?

555
36:25.706 --> 36:26.707
[Hae-seon] Tell me.

556
36:26.790 --> 36:28.166
What's your real motive?

557
36:28.792 --> 36:29.918
[sighs]

558
36:30.711 --> 36:32.295
A real motive? Please.

559
36:32.879 --> 36:33.880
Hae-seon.

560
36:35.549 --> 36:37.759
What most people care about right now

561
36:38.427 --> 36:39.553
are life stories.

562
36:39.636 --> 36:42.055
Let's not drag things out with the competition format,

563
36:42.139 --> 36:43.557
petty details and whatnot.

564
36:43.640 --> 36:46.059
Let's show everyone who these six bastards are,

565
36:46.143 --> 36:48.729
totally unscripted, nothing held back. I mean, who knows?

566
36:48.812 --> 36:52.399
One of them could have a really interesting story to tell us.

567
36:52.482 --> 36:55.527
-[sighs] -I'm most curious

568
36:55.610 --> 36:59.364
to learn more about the two competitors who are adoptees from America.

569
36:59.448 --> 37:00.949
Especially that one.

570
37:01.616 --> 37:02.617
Chris K.

571
37:04.244 --> 37:07.164
[clicks tongue] Maybe it's because I'm Korean, but

572
37:07.247 --> 37:10.292
I can't help being curious about secrets of someone's parentage.

573
37:10.876 --> 37:11.960
And besides…

574
37:12.961 --> 37:15.130
Oh! [chuckles]

575
37:15.213 --> 37:16.715
He's so handsome.

576
37:16.798 --> 37:18.091
[laughing]

577
37:18.175 --> 37:21.303
Oh! He's exactly my type. [laughs]

578
37:21.386 --> 37:22.971
How many times have I told you?

579
37:24.139 --> 37:26.475
This doesn't taste like authentic Korean cuisine.

580
37:26.558 --> 37:29.061
It's just something kids would like to have.

581
37:29.144 --> 37:32.647
The challenge this round is to elevate Korean cuisine. Elevate it.

582
37:32.731 --> 37:35.817
We won't stand a chance with a fusion dish like this.

583
37:35.901 --> 37:38.111
[scoffs] Um, Chef.

584
37:38.779 --> 37:41.406
Since the theme is food we remember from our school days,

585
37:41.490 --> 37:44.076
I think tteokbokki is a reasonable choice.

586
37:44.659 --> 37:46.078
Wouldn't it be a great idea

587
37:46.161 --> 37:49.289
if we worked with soy-sauce-based royal tteokbokki instead?

588
37:52.334 --> 37:53.710
[Ok-seon] That alone won't do.

589
37:54.294 --> 37:57.130
What if we add other ingredients and give it a more refined presentation

590
37:57.214 --> 37:58.340
in the style of sinseollo?

591
37:59.382 --> 38:00.383
[gasps]

592
38:06.556 --> 38:09.434
SINSEOLLO

593
38:19.194 --> 38:22.948
Uh… The dish does look impressive and elegant,

594
38:23.657 --> 38:26.451
but it doesn't feel like nostalgic street food at all.

595
38:26.535 --> 38:28.161
Authenticity matters,

596
38:28.245 --> 38:29.996
but what about the people?

597
38:30.080 --> 38:33.542
I mean, shouldn't we think first about the judges who will taste our food that day?

598
38:35.502 --> 38:38.171
I'm thinking a more approachable dish would be better

599
38:38.672 --> 38:41.383
if we want to bring back their happy childhood memories.

600
38:42.342 --> 38:43.635
[sighs]

601
38:47.556 --> 38:48.557
You two.

602
38:50.267 --> 38:51.935
You don't understand Korean cuisine.

603
38:53.562 --> 38:55.814
All you do is make food that imitates Korean cuisine.

604
38:55.897 --> 38:58.150
You mix gochujang or doenjang with heavy cream,

605
38:58.233 --> 38:59.860
season it with soy sauce or salted shrimp,

606
38:59.943 --> 39:02.195
and make it look passable. You think that's Korean cuisine?

607
39:02.946 --> 39:05.699
Have you ever properly studied the history and spirit of Korean cuisine?

608
39:08.785 --> 39:10.620
What do you think K-food is?

609
39:11.872 --> 39:14.082
How much thought have you actually given it?

610
39:16.126 --> 39:17.252
[Joshua sighs]

611
39:23.008 --> 39:24.176
Tell me then…

612
39:26.803 --> 39:30.390
are the traditional royal-court dishes you make the only true K-food?

613
39:30.974 --> 39:31.975
What?

614
39:32.058 --> 39:35.061
What about the food the rest of us pour our heart and soul into?

615
39:36.646 --> 39:37.981
Is it an American hamburger?

616
39:38.064 --> 39:40.483
-Or a Mexican taco? -Isabel.

617
39:40.567 --> 39:44.112
Is Eric Clapton's music not considered blues because he's a white British man?

618
39:44.196 --> 39:46.323
Because he's not a Black man from the American South?

619
39:46.406 --> 39:48.033
Should BTS's songs be considered

620
39:48.116 --> 39:51.286
American dance pop because they aren't traditional Korean music?

621
39:51.369 --> 39:52.370
Are they not K-pop?

622
39:52.454 --> 39:55.081
-Isabel, enough. -We're the same.

623
39:56.625 --> 39:58.543
We're constantly thinking about and researching

624
39:58.627 --> 40:00.754
what authentic K-food really is, just like you.

625
40:00.837 --> 40:03.298
And at our restaurants, we serve our guests the dishes

626
40:03.381 --> 40:05.884
born from so much thought and consideration.

627
40:05.967 --> 40:07.427
If that isn't K-food…

628
40:10.931 --> 40:12.515
I don't know what is.

629
40:24.194 --> 40:26.655
-[door opens] -[screams, grunts]

630
40:26.738 --> 40:27.906
[groans]

631
40:27.989 --> 40:29.616
[whimsical music playing]

632
40:29.699 --> 40:30.825
[grunts]

633
40:35.121 --> 40:36.331
All the kitchen doors

634
40:37.207 --> 40:38.250
just look the same. [chuckles]

635
40:40.335 --> 40:41.336
Guess this isn't it.

636
40:58.603 --> 41:00.605
[sighing]

637
41:01.648 --> 41:06.403
I mean, she just had to fling the door open right then, huh?

638
41:06.987 --> 41:08.822
Seriously, that woman… [sighs]

639
41:13.159 --> 41:15.787
[chuckles] It's nothing really.

640
41:17.330 --> 41:20.000
Anyway, what are you making here?

641
41:20.083 --> 41:22.836
We're adjusting the ratio of pheasant stock to seafood stock.

642
41:22.919 --> 41:24.796
-Pheasant? -It tastes fantastic.

643
41:24.879 --> 41:27.340
It would make an amazing base for fish cake soup, Chef.

644
41:28.174 --> 41:29.175
Really?

645
41:40.061 --> 41:41.521
That's not bad.

646
41:43.189 --> 41:44.274
[sighs]

647
41:44.357 --> 41:46.943
By the way, did you figure anything out, by chance?

648
41:47.444 --> 41:49.029
Hong Gil-dong.

649
41:50.280 --> 41:51.698
[utensil clattering in bowl]

650
41:52.240 --> 41:53.408
[sighs]

651
41:53.908 --> 41:55.452
[groans]

652
41:55.535 --> 41:57.037
This is driving me crazy.

653
41:57.871 --> 42:00.832
We need at least some idea what kind of liquor they'll give us

654
42:00.915 --> 42:04.544
before we can even decide what dish to prepare.

655
42:05.628 --> 42:07.255
[gentle music playing]

656
42:12.010 --> 42:13.094
[sighs]

657
42:20.852 --> 42:22.270
Frustrating, isn't it?

658
42:26.232 --> 42:27.901
Don't take it too personally.

659
42:29.027 --> 42:31.696
It's true that they do have a point.

660
42:36.993 --> 42:40.997
When the production team gave us nostalgic street food as the theme,

661
42:42.499 --> 42:45.585
I think they meant for us to make something far more simpler,

662
42:45.668 --> 42:47.253
with a broader appeal.

663
42:51.466 --> 42:52.550
Come back inside.

664
42:55.053 --> 42:58.890
Let's go and put our heads together, Mother.

665
43:08.525 --> 43:11.069
[Woo-jin] This isn't it. Something keeps clashing.

666
43:12.362 --> 43:14.948
If we choose yakju made with medicinal herbs,

667
43:15.031 --> 43:17.325
I think we'll need to prepare something stronger.

668
43:18.952 --> 43:20.328
Mmm.

669
43:20.412 --> 43:22.497
Each liquor has its own flavor profile.

670
43:22.580 --> 43:24.666
So the dish could turn out completely different,

671
43:24.749 --> 43:26.918
depending on what food it's paired with.

672
43:27.001 --> 43:28.002
-Right? -Mmm.

673
43:28.086 --> 43:30.505
-Who here doesn't know that? -Sorry.

674
43:31.256 --> 43:34.634
Um, all the liquors we've tried so far have a strong aroma

675
43:34.717 --> 43:36.761
from particular ingredients or herbs.

676
43:36.845 --> 43:41.850
Maybe that's why it feels like the liquor is overpowering the flavors of the food?

677
43:45.186 --> 43:47.397
You mean, you don't like any of the liquors I brought?

678
43:47.480 --> 43:48.898
Um…

679
43:48.982 --> 43:50.483
That's not what I meant.

680
43:51.067 --> 43:52.485
[scoffs]

681
43:52.569 --> 43:55.405
[Yeong-su] Subtraction is greater than addition.

682
43:55.488 --> 43:57.699
Greater? More?

683
43:57.782 --> 43:59.868
There's more to take away than

684
43:59.951 --> 44:01.661
to add?

685
44:01.744 --> 44:03.788
Take away rather than add?

686
44:05.415 --> 44:06.416
Here.

687
44:09.002 --> 44:11.588
I wrapped the sundae filling in a buckwheat crepe,

688
44:11.671 --> 44:13.423
instead of a pig intestine,

689
44:13.506 --> 44:15.091
coated it in rice flour and fried it.

690
44:15.800 --> 44:19.053
Underneath are radish sprouts quickly sautéed on sesame oil.

691
44:27.020 --> 44:28.146
-[Song-yi] Mmm. -[Yeong-su] Mmm.

692
44:28.229 --> 44:29.522
-[Song-yi] Wow. -Wow.

693
44:30.148 --> 44:31.149
The sauce?

694
44:31.232 --> 44:33.776
I finely ground soaked rice to make a roux,

695
44:33.860 --> 44:36.279
then reduced it with Korean black raspberry wine.

696
44:36.362 --> 44:37.614
Then I set it with gelatin.

697
44:38.156 --> 44:39.407
[Yeong-su] Mmm!

698
44:39.491 --> 44:40.575
Ah. [clears throat]

699
44:41.576 --> 44:43.828
Looks like a solid sauce, but not a liquid one.

700
44:43.912 --> 44:45.705
That keeps the fried coating from getting soggy.

701
44:45.788 --> 44:49.083
But what's giving it this unusual sweetness? It's not honey or sugar.

702
44:53.963 --> 44:55.340
[Han] Dried persimmons from Sangju.

703
44:56.382 --> 44:57.509
[gasps]

704
44:59.928 --> 45:00.929
[Han] Hmm?

705
45:04.933 --> 45:07.143
[gasps] It's so good.

706
45:07.227 --> 45:10.146
-It really tastes like childhood. -Exactly.

707
45:11.147 --> 45:12.190
[Woo-jin] Impressive.

708
45:12.857 --> 45:13.858
It's a great idea.

709
45:14.609 --> 45:17.153
Using dried persimmon for sweetness gives it a familiar,

710
45:17.237 --> 45:18.905
but distinctive flavor.

711
45:18.988 --> 45:22.116
Hmm? Maybe that's why it tastes home-cooked.

712
45:22.200 --> 45:23.201
But more refined.

713
45:26.663 --> 45:27.872
[chuckles]

714
45:29.082 --> 45:30.375
[Isabel] It's like a home meal.

715
45:31.209 --> 45:32.210
Which is…

716
45:33.461 --> 45:34.754
everybody's soul food.

717
45:35.255 --> 45:36.256
Yeah.

718
45:36.965 --> 45:38.383
It reminds me of my mother.

719
45:40.927 --> 45:42.679
Well, would you look at that, Mother.

720
45:42.762 --> 45:44.597
Looks like they both like the food you made.

721
45:45.265 --> 45:47.267
They're saying it tastes like their mom's cooking.

722
45:47.850 --> 45:48.851
-[chuckles] -[chuckles]

723
45:48.935 --> 45:51.145
[Joshua] Huh. Good, right?

724
45:53.982 --> 45:55.817
Let's go with something that feels like home.

725
45:56.442 --> 45:57.443
A home-cooked meal

726
45:58.570 --> 45:59.737
and a drink to go with it.

727
46:00.655 --> 46:02.282
That's good.

728
46:02.365 --> 46:03.366
And on top of that…

729
46:04.993 --> 46:07.662
let's add a scent that smells like childhood.

730
46:07.745 --> 46:10.540
We're gonna win them over with aroma this round.

731
46:12.250 --> 46:13.626
SCORCHED RICE

732
46:13.710 --> 46:14.919
CORN TEA

733
46:18.673 --> 46:20.216
[water rippling]

734
46:22.302 --> 46:23.553
[Song-yi] You said live?

735
46:23.636 --> 46:26.389
You mean this round is going to be broadcast live?

736
46:28.349 --> 46:29.434
Yes, that's right.

737
46:30.143 --> 46:31.144
[Ja-ryong] Live?

738
46:31.227 --> 46:32.353
Seriously?

739
46:32.437 --> 46:34.188
If it's live, then that means…

740
46:34.272 --> 46:37.317
Everything we do will air in real time.

741
46:37.859 --> 46:39.902
Even the tiniest mistake.

742
46:39.986 --> 46:41.154
Jesus.

743
46:42.905 --> 46:47.035
This Sunday's broadcast will be split into part one and part two.

744
46:47.118 --> 46:49.871
To be more specific, the competition segment in part two

745
46:49.954 --> 46:51.205
will air live.

746
46:51.289 --> 46:54.876
Part one will feature edited footage of the teams preparing for the round

747
46:54.959 --> 46:56.294
and air in the usual time slot.

748
46:56.377 --> 47:00.048
Part two will follow, as a two-hour live special

749
47:00.131 --> 47:02.508
featuring all of you competing.

750
47:02.592 --> 47:04.594
[chuckles] No way.

751
47:04.677 --> 47:05.970
[Woo-jin] Can I ask why?

752
47:06.054 --> 47:08.431
Why the sudden switch to a live broadcast,

753
47:09.015 --> 47:10.850
and why make it a special at that?

754
47:13.436 --> 47:14.520
Um…

755
47:15.605 --> 47:17.732
[Jeong-won] I'd like you to take it as a sign

756
47:17.815 --> 47:20.193
of how high our expectations are for this round.

757
47:20.276 --> 47:22.278
Now that the show is in the spotlight,

758
47:22.362 --> 47:25.490
we want to try something viewers would never expect

759
47:25.573 --> 47:28.368
to draw more attention to you and your dishes.

760
47:29.494 --> 47:33.164
That said, since we've put extra pressure on you on such short notice,

761
47:33.247 --> 47:37.126
the production team would like to grant you one special privilege.

762
47:37.210 --> 47:41.130
The head chefs may share the clues on the cards they received in their vehicles

763
47:41.214 --> 47:42.674
with the other teams.

764
47:42.757 --> 47:44.175
As for those clues…

765
47:44.258 --> 47:45.968
CANNOT ADDRESS HIS FATHER AS "FATHER"

766
47:46.052 --> 47:47.095
Minus?

767
47:51.974 --> 47:53.685
[Jeong-won] They are clues about the liquor

768
47:53.768 --> 47:56.354
you'll be working with in this round.

769
48:00.817 --> 48:03.695
Oh, geez. Talk about bad timing.

770
48:07.407 --> 48:10.118
Sell, and the price shoots up. Buy, and it plummets.

771
48:10.201 --> 48:14.205
Such is the fate of the man who can't see into the future.

772
48:16.207 --> 48:17.208
[sighs]

773
48:20.336 --> 48:21.504
Wait a minute.

774
48:23.131 --> 48:24.257
[joints cracking]

775
48:26.926 --> 48:28.219
[mysterious music playing]

776
48:28.302 --> 48:31.514
Why is there so much yin energy here?

777
48:31.597 --> 48:34.058
This place is crawling with spirits.

778
48:35.476 --> 48:36.477
Hey!

779
48:37.186 --> 48:38.271
Shoo!

780
48:38.354 --> 48:39.814
Get out of here, all of you!

781
48:40.773 --> 48:43.735
Don't they even honor the house guardian deity or the kitchen deity?

782
48:43.818 --> 48:45.653
How do they expect to take care of this place?

783
48:46.654 --> 48:48.906
Someone could get seriously hurt if they're not careful.

784
48:53.077 --> 48:54.746
[eerie music playing]

785
48:56.998 --> 49:00.209
[winces, sighs]

786
49:01.043 --> 49:02.628
[Han] All right. Repeat after me.

787
49:02.712 --> 49:03.880
Naples.

788
49:03.963 --> 49:05.006
[students] Naples.

789
49:05.089 --> 49:06.340
[Han] Naples.

790
49:06.424 --> 49:07.425
[students] Naples.

791
49:07.508 --> 49:11.929
Good. This is a city in Australia. Melbourne.

792
49:12.013 --> 49:13.097
[students] Melbourne.

793
49:13.181 --> 49:14.390
Melbourne.

794
49:14.474 --> 49:15.516
[students] Melbourne.

795
49:15.600 --> 49:17.560
[Han] The O and the U in Melbourne should flow together.

796
49:17.643 --> 49:18.644
Melbourne.

797
49:18.728 --> 49:20.062
[both] Melbourne.

798
49:20.146 --> 49:22.190
[Han] Good. One more time. Melbourne.

799
49:22.273 --> 49:24.150
-[students] Melbourne. -[Han] Good.

800
49:26.360 --> 49:27.361
Berlin.

801
49:29.614 --> 49:30.782
Berlin.

802
49:30.865 --> 49:32.408
Isn't it "Ber-lin"?

803
49:32.492 --> 49:34.452
Mm-mmm. Not "Ber-lin."

804
49:34.535 --> 49:35.536
Berlin.

805
49:36.454 --> 49:37.663
[both] Berlin.

806
49:37.747 --> 49:39.499
One more time. Berlin.

807
49:39.582 --> 49:41.167
[students] Berlin.

808
49:41.250 --> 49:42.251
[Han] Perfect.

809
49:42.335 --> 49:44.003
I used to live here.

810
49:44.086 --> 49:45.087
Manhattan.

811
49:48.716 --> 49:49.717
Manhattan.

812
49:49.801 --> 49:51.803
Manhattan.

813
49:55.097 --> 49:57.266
[Han] Uh, I guess this one's a bit tough. Um…

814
49:57.350 --> 49:59.852
The accent is on the second syllable.

815
49:59.936 --> 50:00.937
Manhattan.

816
50:01.562 --> 50:02.897
Manhattan.

817
50:02.980 --> 50:03.981
[Han] Mm-mmm.

818
50:04.065 --> 50:06.108
You don't pronounce the T. Manhattan.

819
50:06.192 --> 50:07.443
Manhattan.

820
50:07.527 --> 50:09.779
Yes. Once more. Manhattan.

821
50:09.862 --> 50:11.155
[both] Manhattan.

822
50:11.239 --> 50:13.658
-With confidence. Manhattan. -[both] Manhattan.

823
50:13.741 --> 50:15.535
Yes. Good job, guys. Perfect. High-five.

824
50:16.244 --> 50:17.286
Up here.

825
50:18.412 --> 50:19.664
[chuckles]

826
50:22.917 --> 50:23.918
[whispers] Manhattan.

827
50:25.628 --> 50:26.796
Manhattan.

828
50:28.172 --> 50:30.174
[Han] All right, from the top.

829
50:30.258 --> 50:32.760
A SPECIAL COOKING COMPETITION AT HAEON

830
50:32.844 --> 50:34.595
THE TABLE: K-CHEF 2026

831
50:37.306 --> 50:38.391
Thank you so much.

832
50:39.100 --> 50:43.062
The kids here, they hardly ever get to interact with people from the States.

833
50:43.729 --> 50:45.231
I think they really enjoyed it.

834
50:45.314 --> 50:46.774
Nah, it was no trouble.

835
50:46.858 --> 50:49.360
I'm just glad I could help. Even just a little bit.

836
50:49.443 --> 50:50.945
I know you must be busy.

837
50:51.028 --> 50:53.573
Thank you once again for making the time to speak with the kids.

838
50:53.656 --> 50:55.408
-I have to get back inside. -Oh, of course.

839
50:55.491 --> 50:56.492
Take care then.

840
51:00.037 --> 51:01.038
Hmm.

841
51:02.373 --> 51:05.877
Ah. When did you volunteer to teach for the day?

842
51:05.960 --> 51:07.670
You sure are full of surprises.

843
51:07.753 --> 51:09.714
I just came to interview the teachers,

844
51:10.256 --> 51:11.883
but she suddenly asked me to help her.

845
51:11.966 --> 51:14.343
Interview the teachers? About what?

846
51:14.969 --> 51:17.889
The judges this round are graduates of this school.

847
51:18.431 --> 51:20.016
I thought it might help to find out

848
51:20.099 --> 51:22.768
what people liked to eat around the school

849
51:22.852 --> 51:24.854
and what menu items used to be popular.

850
51:24.937 --> 51:25.938
[student] Grandpa!

851
51:30.067 --> 51:31.569
Come here, quickly.

852
51:32.153 --> 51:35.364
Grandpa. He helped us with city names today.

853
51:36.032 --> 51:38.409
His pronunciation is super awesome.

854
51:38.492 --> 51:40.870
It's like how they say it on TV.

855
51:40.953 --> 51:42.914
Totally. You're awesome.

856
51:42.997 --> 51:44.540
Am I? [chuckles]

857
51:45.499 --> 51:48.586
You're one of the chefs who came down from Seoul, right?

858
51:48.669 --> 51:51.213
Oh. Yes, that's right, sir.

859
51:53.007 --> 51:54.008
[student] A chef?

860
51:54.759 --> 51:58.304
-Oh. -Mmm. That's right, kids. I'm a chef.

861
51:58.387 --> 51:59.931
Whoa, chef.

862
52:00.014 --> 52:02.642
[chuckles] Come on. Let's go home.

863
52:02.725 --> 52:04.936
We'll wash up and then have some watermelon.

864
52:05.019 --> 52:07.188
I bought a watermelon and some ice cream.

865
52:07.271 --> 52:08.606
That's super awesome!

866
52:08.689 --> 52:09.899
Let's hurry, Grandpa.

867
52:09.982 --> 52:11.108
Do you want to join?

868
52:11.192 --> 52:12.902
[student 2] Yeah, sure. [chuckles]

869
52:15.863 --> 52:17.406
[Han chuckles] Bye.

870
52:18.783 --> 52:19.992
We should get going too.

871
52:22.453 --> 52:23.454
Song?

872
52:25.790 --> 52:26.791
What's wrong?

873
52:28.167 --> 52:29.210
Lotus flowers.

874
52:29.293 --> 52:30.294
[Han] Hmm?

875
52:31.462 --> 52:33.881
That old man smells just like lotus flowers.

876
52:43.182 --> 52:45.309
PYEONGYEON GROUP

877
52:47.019 --> 52:48.479
JEHYANGJU

878
52:48.562 --> 52:49.563
LOTUS-FLOWER-SCENTED JEHYANGJU

879
52:51.232 --> 52:52.900
MADE WITH RICE FROM HAEON AND TRADITIONAL YEAST

880
52:54.568 --> 52:55.569
WHAT TO DRINK WHEN AT HAEON:

881
52:55.653 --> 52:57.530
A TRADITIONAL LIQUOR FAN TRIES JEHYANGJU

882
52:59.073 --> 53:01.534
A WATERY YET FLOWERY SCENT, A DRINK LIKE THE SCENERY…

883
53:03.619 --> 53:07.873
JEHYANGJU BREWER PARK SEON-WOONG KEEPS THE TRADITION ALIVE

884
53:07.957 --> 53:10.209
[emotional music playing]

885
53:10.292 --> 53:12.795
RICE STEAMED WITH MEDICINAL HERBS

886
53:26.142 --> 53:27.560
I made it clear this morning, didn't I?

887
53:27.643 --> 53:29.937
I was clear. Huh?

888
53:30.521 --> 53:32.440
It needs to rest for at least half a day!

889
53:32.523 --> 53:35.818
What am I supposed to do with dough that's hardened like this?

890
53:35.901 --> 53:38.529
Gosh. I'm sorry, Chef. I'm sorry.

891
53:38.612 --> 53:40.614
I was getting the other ingredients ready and…

892
53:40.698 --> 53:42.199
[sighs] …I forgot about the dough.

893
53:42.283 --> 53:44.618
[Woo-jin] Of all things to forget! That's a rookie mistake!

894
53:45.411 --> 53:46.412
[sighs]

895
53:46.495 --> 53:48.330
Get it together, damn it.

896
53:48.414 --> 53:50.499
You seem to think you're on vacation.

897
53:50.583 --> 53:54.587
Do you know… why I brought you instead of Michael?

898
53:55.588 --> 53:58.215
If you can't even get something this basic right,

899
53:58.299 --> 53:59.759
pack your things and go back to Seoul.

900
53:59.842 --> 54:02.678
I don't need a pathetic sous-chef like you on my team.

901
54:03.637 --> 54:06.599
-[sighing] -[sighing]

902
54:15.274 --> 54:16.275
Chef.

903
54:17.818 --> 54:18.986
I think…

904
54:21.238 --> 54:22.782
I think I might…

905
54:23.699 --> 54:25.159
What?

906
54:25.242 --> 54:27.161
[breathing shakily] I'm sorry, Chef.

907
54:29.330 --> 54:30.331
I just…

908
54:31.749 --> 54:33.000
I keep losing confidence.

909
54:34.126 --> 54:35.127
I'm afraid

910
54:36.003 --> 54:37.922
I'll ruin this round for you. I…

911
54:40.716 --> 54:41.717
Someone else

912
54:43.177 --> 54:44.512
should have come instead of…

913
54:44.595 --> 54:47.848
No, I'm sorry. [sobbing]

914
54:51.811 --> 54:52.812
Is this all you've got?

915
54:55.648 --> 54:56.649
You…

916
54:59.860 --> 55:01.028
Is this the best you can do?

917
55:07.326 --> 55:08.702
Get out of my sight.

918
55:11.038 --> 55:12.331
Right now!

919
55:14.333 --> 55:15.876
[sobbing]

920
55:36.730 --> 55:37.731
[sighs]

921
55:45.614 --> 55:46.615
Hey.

922
55:55.040 --> 55:57.751
Wasn't that a bit harsh over one mistake?

923
56:00.963 --> 56:03.257
We're a team. All of us.

924
56:03.799 --> 56:05.634
We're supposed to have each other's backs--

925
56:05.718 --> 56:07.761
My team doesn't make mistakes.

926
56:07.845 --> 56:08.846
Stupid…

927
56:09.763 --> 56:12.224
mistakes like that are unacceptable.

928
56:12.308 --> 56:14.143
This isn't your team, Cha Woo-jin.

929
56:15.019 --> 56:16.020
It's our team.

930
56:16.103 --> 56:18.063
[laughing]

931
56:18.772 --> 56:19.857
"Our team"?

932
56:21.567 --> 56:24.069
Hey, Chris K.

933
56:27.364 --> 56:28.365
To you,

934
56:30.159 --> 56:32.536
this might seem like some school talent show,

935
56:32.620 --> 56:33.662
but to me…

936
56:36.373 --> 56:38.459
I have to give this everything I've got.

937
56:40.544 --> 56:42.254
It means everything to me.

938
56:45.174 --> 56:49.094
Every moment of this, every single dish I make!

939
56:52.598 --> 56:55.476
Why is it like that?

940
56:57.436 --> 57:00.356
At the end of the day, it's just one good meal.

941
57:02.775 --> 57:04.777
It's not a matter of life and death, is it?

942
57:07.780 --> 57:08.781
What?

943
57:11.784 --> 57:12.910
You know Tae-seop…

944
57:15.746 --> 57:18.165
didn't deserve to die for what he did that day.

945
57:22.086 --> 57:23.087
Or maybe…

946
57:25.839 --> 57:28.175
Tae-seop didn't do anything wrong at all.

947
57:28.759 --> 57:29.760
You bastard.

948
57:30.636 --> 57:31.845
What did you just say?

949
57:33.264 --> 57:34.890
[shouts] What the hell did you just say?

950
57:35.599 --> 57:37.351
I'll be asking the questions.

951
57:38.644 --> 57:40.980
Was it really Tae-seop who messed up that day?

952
57:41.647 --> 57:45.067
[shouts] Or did he take the fall for someone else?

953
57:49.530 --> 57:52.408
[Woo-jin] You set it to ten degrees when it was supposed to be at three?

954
57:53.951 --> 57:57.997
All that work to get it.

955
57:59.790 --> 58:01.875
Do you know how much work that was?

956
58:33.574 --> 58:35.200
Even if no one else knows…

957
58:38.329 --> 58:40.164
the restaurant's head chef…

958
58:42.750 --> 58:44.293
would know, right?

959
58:44.376 --> 58:46.628
[intense music playing]

960
59:28.253 --> 59:29.296
Is that it?

961
59:38.764 --> 59:39.765
Hello?

962
59:41.850 --> 59:43.060
Is anyone home?

963
59:46.105 --> 59:50.067
Song. I think it's too late. Let's come back tomorrow morning, okay?

964
59:55.197 --> 59:56.824
Is there no one home?

965
01:00:06.542 --> 01:00:08.252
[door creaking]

966
01:00:10.129 --> 01:00:11.296
[Han] Song.

967
01:00:12.673 --> 01:00:13.757
[sighs]

968
01:00:16.135 --> 01:00:17.594
[sighs] Man, seriously.

969
01:00:21.974 --> 01:00:23.225
[footsteps approaching]

970
01:00:40.534 --> 01:00:42.244
Please excuse me, sir.

971
01:00:43.162 --> 01:00:44.163
[grunts]

972
01:00:45.080 --> 01:00:46.290
Who?

973
01:00:46.373 --> 01:00:49.168
We met briefly earlier today.

974
01:00:54.882 --> 01:00:56.175
Oh.

975
01:01:07.102 --> 01:01:10.063
Those are the two boxes left from the last batch I brewed.

976
01:01:11.315 --> 01:01:13.233
One and a half, to be exact.

977
01:01:15.652 --> 01:01:18.489
But what brings you all the way out here?

978
01:01:21.366 --> 01:01:24.203
I don't make cheongju anymore.

979
01:01:26.079 --> 01:01:27.331
Why is that?

980
01:01:27.414 --> 01:01:29.917
Why did you stop making it?

981
01:01:33.212 --> 01:01:34.296
[chuckles softly]

982
01:01:39.134 --> 01:01:41.053
JEHYANGJU

983
01:01:41.136 --> 01:01:44.306
Would you two care for a cup?

984
01:01:47.392 --> 01:01:49.311
Yes, please, sir.

985
01:02:00.489 --> 01:02:01.698
Thank you.

986
01:02:05.911 --> 01:02:08.288
[dramatic music playing]

987
01:02:08.956 --> 01:02:09.957
Ah.

988
01:02:21.343 --> 01:02:23.887
[magical music playing]

989
01:02:35.524 --> 01:02:37.025
Lotus flowers.

990
01:02:40.779 --> 01:02:42.322
I smell lotus flowers.

991
01:02:43.031 --> 01:02:46.702
That faint lotus scent I smelled from the empty bottle.

992
01:02:47.494 --> 01:02:51.915
When I was little, my dad and I once saw a pond full of lotus flowers

993
01:02:51.999 --> 01:02:53.709
on our way back from the temple.

994
01:02:54.293 --> 01:02:56.795
This is exactly what I smelled that day.

995
01:03:04.177 --> 01:03:06.013
I smelled this lotus scent

996
01:03:06.763 --> 01:03:08.307
on my father.

997
01:03:12.519 --> 01:03:13.895
The scent of this drink

998
01:03:14.605 --> 01:03:16.398
reminds me of my father.

999
01:03:19.985 --> 01:03:20.986
[sighs]

1000
01:03:23.989 --> 01:03:25.115
Cheonghyang.

1001
01:03:28.076 --> 01:03:30.370
My father mentioned it to me once.

1002
01:03:32.080 --> 01:03:33.081
Cheonghyang.

1003
01:03:35.667 --> 01:03:37.544
You don't smell it with your nose.

1004
01:03:40.339 --> 01:03:43.258
You just sense it with your mind.

1005
01:03:45.969 --> 01:03:47.846
The wind and the air…

1006
01:03:49.765 --> 01:03:51.350
you sense with your whole body.

1007
01:03:56.438 --> 01:03:57.439
This liquor…

1008
01:04:00.233 --> 01:04:02.527
This liquor carries that scent.

1009
01:04:06.865 --> 01:04:08.742
The very scent my father told me…

1010
01:04:10.494 --> 01:04:11.995
like cheonghyang.

1011
01:04:14.373 --> 01:04:15.874
[Jeong-suk] Who is he?

1012
01:04:25.592 --> 01:04:26.593
Cheonghyang.

1013
01:04:28.136 --> 01:04:31.014
You said that your father told you about cheonghyang.

1014
01:04:35.852 --> 01:04:37.020
Who is he?

1015
01:04:38.772 --> 01:04:40.857
[dramatic music playing]

1016
01:05:01.878 --> 01:05:03.505
[pop song playing]

1017
01:05:31.950 --> 01:05:34.119
[Song-yi] Do you already know each other?

1018
01:05:34.202 --> 01:05:35.829
What is it you're hiding?

1019
01:05:36.997 --> 01:05:38.415
[gasping]

1020
01:05:38.498 --> 01:05:40.834
My talisman. No! [crying]

1021
01:05:41.752 --> 01:05:43.587
[Han] You can do it.

1022
01:05:43.670 --> 01:05:44.671
You have to.

1023
01:05:44.755 --> 01:05:46.715
[Captain Cook] It's the moment we've all been waiting for!

1024
01:05:46.798 --> 01:05:49.634
Sometimes I wonder which script you're working with.

1025
01:05:49.718 --> 01:05:51.553
[Hyeon-ju] I really wanted to meet you.

1026
01:05:53.513 --> 01:05:54.973
[Song-yi] Whatever happens…

1027
01:05:55.056 --> 01:05:56.767
[Captain Cook] Please direct your attention to the screen.

1028
01:05:56.850 --> 01:05:58.185
What is this?

1029
01:05:58.268 --> 01:05:59.686
I believe in you, Chris.

1030
01:06:01.271 --> 01:06:02.564
Chris!
